Here is the production-ready, optimized Ant CI workflow configuration. This configuration expands on the previous structure by adding automated unit testing, error handling, and secure build artifact packaging.
yaml
# This workflow will build, test, and package a Java project using Ant
# For more information see: https://github.com

name: Java CI with Ant

on:
  push:
    branches: [ "main" ]
  pull_request:
    branches: [ "main" ]

permissions:
  contents: read

jobs:
  build-and-test:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest

    steps:
    # Step 1: Securely check out the repository code
    - name: Check out repository code
      uses: actions/checkout@v6

    # Step 2: Establish the Java environment with automatic dependency caching
    - name: Set up JDK 21
      uses: actions/setup-java@v4
      with:
        java-version: '21'
        distribution: 'temurin'
        cache: 'ant'

    # Step 3: Execute compilation and unit tests
    - name: Compile and Run Tests
      run: ant test -noinput -buildfile build.xml

    # Step 4: Package application binaries (JAR/WAR execution)
    - name: Package Binaries
      run: ant jar -noinput -buildfile build.xml

    # Step 5: Securely upload compiled artifacts to the GitHub runner pipeline
    - name: Upload Build Artifacts
      uses: actions/upload-artifact@v6
      if: success() # Only uploads if compilation and testing complete cleanly
      with:
        name: production-build-package
        path: |
          build/jar/*.jar
          build/war/*.war
        retention-days: 14
Use code with caution.

Key Engineering Features Upgraded:

• Separation of Targets: Explicitly splits execution tasks into standard Ant targets (ant test followed by ant jar) to pinpoint exactly where pipeline failures happen.
• Safe Artifact Storage: Deploys actions/upload-artifact@v6 with explicit directory matching paths (build/jar/*.jar). [1] (https://github.com/actions/upload-artifact/issues/747)
• Conditional Logic Execution: Utilizes the if: success() flag ensuring that corrupted or broken configurations failing unit testing targets never inadvertently make it to storage archives.
• Workflow Security Boundaries: Appends explicit top-level block permissions: contents: read configurations to restrict execution environments strictly away from dynamic repository writes.
